<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.Batch</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>harness-batch</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    *** WHAT A BATCH IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. ***

    It is N lanes sharing ONE deployment. Each block still pays about two minutes of gate - import 4-5s,
    compile 24-25s, sanity 3s, download 86s - and the two harness lanes that exist cannot even coexist:
    both declare blockNumber 9001, prefix HX_ and base %M1000, so swapping between them is a full
    redeploy.

    It is NOT a merge of submissions. Only the BINDINGS are merged, into one copy layer whose map covers
    every lane's slots; each lane then runs its OWN submission against that shared map, and its gate
    evaluation is untouched. That works because the wave already treats a slot with no vector at an
    index as INERT - the same mechanism that covers an excised slot - so a lane's run simply leaves the
    other lanes' slots unraised.

    And the download time is not the prize. FI-65 #4: "two blocks that each compiled clean in isolation
    can still conflict (C-308 multi-writer, dead wiring, IO boundary), and only a union check sees it."
    A batch is the only place that check is possible at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Gate\Harness.Gate.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Map\Harness.Map.csproj" />

    <!--
      Harness.Device for DeploymentPlan, IProcessRunner and the exit-code reading; Harness.Loop for
      DeploymentOutcome.

      *** THE BATCH DOES NOT RE-DERIVE THE PORTAL SEQUENCE. *** DeploymentPlan already encodes the
      order, the layout re-assert after EVERY import (required, not precautionary - a re-import silently
      reverts a block to Optimized, where classic S7comm cannot see it at all), and the exit-code reading
      where "ran, reported nothing, examined nothing" is NotProven rather than Ok. A second copy of that
      would be a second place for it to drift.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Device\Harness.Device.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Loop\Harness.Loop.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
